# Cursor Hooks - Layout and Conventions

Cursor agent hooks (`afterFileEdit`, `beforeShellExecution`, `beforeReadFile`, `sessionStart`, `stop`,
...) are configured in [.cursor/hooks.json](mdc:.cursor/hooks.json). To keep hooks readable, debuggable
and version-control friendly, this project keeps the **logic in standalone shell scripts** under
`.cursor/hooks/` and limits `hooks.json` to short pointers. Do **not** inline multi-statement commands
into `hooks.json`.

This mirrors the Claude Code convention documented in
[.claude/rules/claude-code-hooks.md](mdc:.claude/rules/claude-code-hooks.md). Read that file too - the
directory-per-event layout, kebab-case action-verb file naming, mandatory header comment, "no silent
failures" rule, and the "`.sh` entry point; paired `.ts` helper when a hook needs real logic" shape all
apply here unchanged. Only the differences below are Cursor-specific.

## Directory Layout

```
.cursor/
  hooks.json
  hooks/
    afterFileEdit/
      <descriptive-kebab-case-name>.sh
    beforeShellExecution/
      <descriptive-kebab-case-name>.sh
    ...
```

One subdirectory per Cursor hook event, named **exactly** as the event appears in `hooks.json`
(`afterFileEdit`, `afterShellExecution`, `beforeMCPExecution`, `beforeReadFile`,
`beforeShellExecution`, `preCompact`, `sessionEnd`, `sessionStart`, `stop`, ...). Cursor's event names
are camelCase - do not PascalCase them to match the `.claude/hooks/` directories.

## Differences From the Claude Code Hooks (Deliberate - Do Not "Fix" These)

A `cmd-sync-ai-instructions-*` pass will notice these divergences. They are intentional:

- **Flat `file_path` in the payload.** Cursor's `afterFileEdit` event carries `file_path` at the TOP
  level; the Claude Code `PostToolUse` event nests the same value under `tool_input`. A hook copied
  from one tree to the other must have this changed or it silently never fires.
- **`afterFileEdit` is informational-only.** Cursor cannot be blocked from it, so these hooks leave the
  exit code at `0` and report problems on stderr. The Claude Code `PostToolUse` counterpart exits `2`
  on failure.
- **Paths resolved from the script, not an env var.** Cursor sets no `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, and its
  hook cwd and PATH are less specified than Claude Code's, so resolve everything from the script's own
  location (`cd "$(dirname "$0")"`) and invoke project scripts by path rather than through
  `node --run`.

Like the `.claude` hooks, each script starts with `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and must stay executable
(mode 755) - Cursor's `command` value is a bare script path and is not guaranteed to be run through a
shell. Nothing in the repo enforces the mode, so a lost `+x` here breaks the hook silently.

## Payload

Every hook receives the event JSON on stdin - read it whole (`grep` reads it directly in a shell hook;
a paired `.ts` helper reads `readFileSync(0, 'utf8')`). The common fields are
`conversation_id`, `generation_id`, `model`, `hook_event_name`, `cursor_version`, `workspace_roots`,
`user_email` and `transcript_path`. `afterFileEdit` adds:

```json
{
    "file_path": "<absolute path>",
    "edits": [{ "old_string": "<search>", "new_string": "<replace>" }]
}
```

**Gating on `file_path` from a shell hook.** The payload carries the edit strings too, so a naive
`grep` for `"file_path"` also matches a file whose *contents* mention it - and that is not
hypothetical, since the smoke-test line below is exactly such a string. Anchor the match on `[{,]` so
it can only hit a real JSON key: inside a JSON string the same text is escaped as `\"file_path\"`, and
the required unescaped quote then cannot match.

```sh
grep -Eq '[{,][[:space:]]*"file_path"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]*[/\\])?(package\.json\.ts|utils[/\\]package-json-utils[/\\][^"]*\.ts)"'
```

## Wiring Into `hooks.json`

Each entry is a thin pointer. Paths are relative to the repo root:

```json
{
    "version": 1,
    "hooks": {
        "afterFileEdit": [
            {
                "command": ".cursor/hooks/afterFileEdit/<hook-name>.sh"
            }
        ]
    }
}
```

Use `.cursor/hooks.json` - **not** `.cursor/.hooks.json`. Never ship both: if a future Cursor version
reads both, every hook fires twice, which for the package.json regenerator would mean two generator
processes racing to write the same file.

## Smoke-Testing a Hook

Cursor reads hooks at session start, so reload the window after adding or editing one, and check
Cursor Settings > Hooks if it does not fire. Before that, verify the file itself by piping a
representative event into it:

```sh
echo '{"file_path":"'"$PWD"'/package.json.ts","edits":[]}' | ./.cursor/hooks/afterFileEdit/regenerate-package-json-after-source-edit.sh
```

Then confirm the no-op path with a non-matching `file_path` (it should print nothing and exit `0`).

## Current Hooks

- [.cursor/hooks/afterFileEdit/regenerate-package-json-after-source-edit.sh](mdc:.cursor/hooks/afterFileEdit/regenerate-package-json-after-source-edit.sh) -
  regenerates `package.json` whenever the agent edits `package.json.ts` or
  `utils/package-json-utils/*.ts`. Counterpart of the Claude Code PostToolUse hook and of the
  folder-open watcher task in [.vscode/tasks.json](mdc:.vscode/tasks.json), which covers plain human
  edits.

Note that Cursor has no `beforeFileEdit` event, so the Claude Code guard that blocks direct
`package.json` edits (`.claude/hooks/PreToolUse/block-direct-package-json-edit.sh`) has no exact Cursor
counterpart. The health check `pkg-json-sync` (`node --run test:compare-package-json-with-source`) is
the backstop.
